<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bernhard III, Duke of Saxe-Meiningen</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1851">1851</a>–<a href="/years/1928">1928</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bernhard III, Duke of Saxe-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiningen">Meiningen</a>, was the last reigning duke of Saxe-Meiningen, succeeding his father George II in 1914. A Prussian general and a scholar of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, he was married to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princess-charlotte-of-wales">Princess Charlotte</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a>. He abdicated in November 1918 during the German Revolution, and the duchy became a free state that joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> in 1920.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bernhard-iii-duke-of-saxe-meiningen.jpg" alt="Bernhard III, Duke of Saxe-Meiningen" width="320" height="501" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Louis Otto Weber · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bernhard_III_(Saxe-Meiningen).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
